---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import { doc, guides, releases, repo } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['install', '1. Install'],
  ['sign-in', '2. Sign in'],
  ['first-task', '3. Run a first task'],
  ['extend', '4. Add capabilities'],
  ['trust', 'Permissions and data'],
  ['help', 'Troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Install and start"
  description="From a fresh install to your first reviewable task. Start with one provider, then add the extensions and services you need."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>1. Install the engine and desktop</h2>
    <p>
      You need Node.js 22.19 or newer and a provider account or API key. Install pi in a terminal:
    </p>
    <pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install pi"><code>npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent</code></pre>
    <p>
      <a href={`${releases}/latest`}>Download Phosphor from the latest release</a>. On macOS, open
      the DMG and drag Phosphor to Applications. On Linux, choose the AppImage or .deb. On Windows,
      run the x64 .exe installer.
    </p>
    <p>Alternatively, on macOS or Linux:</p>
    <pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install Phosphor on macOS or Linux"><code>curl -fsSL https://github.com/agustinsacco/Phosphor/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p>
      The script verifies release checksums. Piping a script to a shell is a trust decision: <a
        href={`${repo}/blob/main/scripts/install.sh`}>read the installer first</a
      >. Windows releases are unsigned; SmartScreen may require More info → Run anyway after you
      verify the source.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Check:</strong> launch Phosphor. If the setup screen says pi is missing, make sure <code
        >pi --version</code
      > works and that its directory is on the PATH available to desktop apps. Restart Phosphor after
      changing PATH.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="sign-in">
    <h2>2. Choose a sign-in route</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>ChatGPT Plus/Pro:</strong> Settings → Accounts → sign in to the ChatGPT/Codex provider.
        This uses pi’s native OAuth; a Codex CLI bridge is not needed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude Pro/Max:</strong> install the official Claude Code CLI and the pi provider package,
        then manage logins under Settings → Extensions → Claude Code. <a href="/guides/claude-code/"
          >Follow the screenshot guide</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub Copilot or Kimi For Coding:</strong> use the corresponding sign-in in Settings
        → Accounts. Copilot Enterprise Server uses pi’s terminal login.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>API keys or custom endpoints:</strong> configure the provider in pi. <a
          href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/providers.md"
          >Provider authentication</a
        > and <a
          href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/models.md"
          >custom model configuration</a
        > describe the available routes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a graphical sign-in cannot complete, open a terminal, run <code>pi</code>, and use <code
        >/login</code
      >. Claude Code logins are separate from pi’s provider credentials. Provider billing rules and
      plan limits apply; installing Phosphor does not include model usage.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="first-task">
    <h2>3. Run one task you can verify</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open a repository folder you trust. Use a disposable project for your first experiment.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose a model in the composer. For Claude subscription usage, select the <code
          >pi-claude-cli</code
        > provider, not an API-billed Anthropic entry.
      </li>
      <li>
        Leave the new-worktree option enabled for a separate checkout, or deliberately choose the
        main checkout. Git features require a Git repository.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ask for a bounded change, for example: “Add a test for an empty input in this parser. Run
        the relevant tests and show me the diff.”
      </li>
      <li>
        Open tool activity to inspect the command and its result. Open Changes to review each file.
        An agent saying “done” is not proof that its tests passed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ask for a short explanation as an artifact if you want a versioned document beside the chat.
        Use Files or Terminal when you want to inspect or run something yourself.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Check:</strong> you can identify the session’s branch, read its actual diff, and inspect
      the test result. For a second independent task, start a new lane and worktree rather than asking
      two sessions to edit the same checkout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('worktrees.md')}>Worktree safeguards</a> · <a href={doc('chat.md')}
        >Transcript and composer reference</a
      > · <a href={doc('routines.md')}>Schedule a repeatable task with a local routine</a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="extend">
    <h2>4. Add the capabilities you need</h2>
    <p>
      A working coding session is the starting point. pi packages add web research, MCP services,
      subagents, and desktop interaction. Install only what serves your workflow, then start a new
      session.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      {
        guides.slice(1).map(({ href, title }) => (
          <a href={href}>
            <h3>{title} →</h3>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="trust">
    <h2>Permissions and data</h2>
    <p>
      Phosphor runs locally, but that does not mean inference is local. Model providers receive the
      prompts and tool results sent to them. Connected services have their own permissions and data
      policies.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tools run with full permissions by default.</strong> Phosphor does not add an approval system
      or a universal agent sandbox. Worktrees separate Git checkouts, not filesystem or network access.
      Packages execute code in pi’s process. Review package sources and connect only services you intend
      to expose to the agent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Model-authored HTML artifacts are isolated from the app and have no network access. That
      boundary protects against the document, not against the agent’s tools. <a
        href={doc('extensions.md')}>Read the extension and artifact contract</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="help">
    <h2>If setup stalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>pi missing:</strong> check the desktop PATH, not only an interactive terminal’s PATH.
      </li><li>
        <strong>No models:</strong> confirm authentication and start a new session after changing provider
        configuration.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Claude is unavailable:</strong> use the <a
          href="/guides/claude-code/#troubleshooting">Claude troubleshooting checklist</a
        >.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Connector cannot authenticate:</strong> check scopes and run <a
          href="/guides/connectors/#verify">Test in MCP Connectors</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('updates.md')}>Install-specific update behavior</a> · <a href={`${repo}/issues`}
        >Report a problem</a
      >. Include versions and the error, but remove tokens, account identifiers, private paths, and
      sensitive project content from logs and screenshots.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
